     "type": "read",
     "title": "Podcast transcript: “Why your English sounds too perfect”",
     "img": "l8_podcast",
     "text": "Okay, so today I want to talk about something that, honestly, nobody told me when I was learning English. I came to the States from Brazil about ten years ago, and my grammar was great. Like, really great. I could explain the difference between every tense. But people kept telling me I sounded “formal”. One coworker actually asked me if I was angry, which, you know, I wasn’t!\n\nIt took me a while to figure out what was going on. I was speaking in perfect, complete sentences. “Yes, I agree with your proposal. I will send the document tomorrow.” Correct, right? But it sounded like a robot reading an email. My American coworkers would say something like, “Yeah, no, totally, I’ll send it over tomorrow.” Which, if you think about it, makes no sense. Yeah, no? But it sounds friendly.\n\nSo here’s what I started doing. First, I listened. I paid attention to the little words, not the big ones. “So.” “Anyway.” “I mean.” “Kind of.” I wrote them in a notebook, and I tried to use one or two in every conversation. It felt fake at first, I’m not gonna lie. But after a few months, it just became normal.\n\nSecond, I stopped being afraid of pauses. Before, when I didn’t know a word, I would freeze and my face would go red. Now I just say, “Hmm, what’s the word… the thing you use to…” and people jump in and help. Americans love helping, by the way.\n\nAnd third, and this is the big one, I learned to soften things. In Portuguese, we can be pretty direct. Here, if you say “That’s wrong,” people hear “You’re stupid.” If you say “Hmm, I could be wrong, but I think…”, people hear “Let’s figure this out together.” Same information, totally different feeling.\n\nAnyway, long story short: fluent doesn’t mean perfect. Fluent means relaxed. And that’s something you can practice, starting today.",
     "gloss": {
      "figure out": "understand after thinking about it",
      "send it over": "send it (casual)",
      "jump in": "join a conversation quickly",
      "go red": "become red in the face (embarrassment)"
     },
