     "type": "read",
     "title": "Review: “Hidden gem in Seminole Heights”",
     "img": "l8_espresso",
     "text": "I almost didn’t write this review, because honestly, I want to keep this place to myself. But the owners deserve it.\n\nFirst, the location. It’s on a quiet side street, so you’ll probably drive past it the first time. Parking is limited, and on weekends it fills up fast, so either come early or be ready to walk a couple of blocks. Totally worth it.\n\nThe coffee is the real star. If you like strong coffee, order the cortadito. It’s rich, a little sweet, and it’ll keep you going all morning. My husband, who usually takes his coffee with a ton of milk and sugar, was converted on the first sip. The pastries are made in-house every morning, and the guava and cheese one is out of this world.\n\nNow, a few things to be fully aware of before you go. Prices are on the high side: two coffees and two pastries came to almost twenty-five dollars before tip. It’s not a rip-off, because the quality is excellent, but it’s not your everyday stop either. Also, the Wi-Fi is weak, so if you’re planning to get some work done, it might not be the best choice.\n\nService was warm and friendly. We showed up during a heavy downpour, soaking wet, and the barista handed us paper towels before we even ordered. Little things like that make a big difference.\n\nBottom line: if you appreciate good coffee and don’t mind paying a little more, I highly recommend it. It’s a tough call between this place and my old favorite downtown, but I think I have a new go-to.\n\n★★★★★",
