     "title": "Essay: “The year I almost didn’t move”",
     "img": "l8_beach_sunset",
     "text": "Three years ago, I almost said no to Tampa.\n\nMy company offered me a transfer from Chicago, and my first reaction was panic. I didn’t know anyone in Florida. I had a great apartment, a favorite coffee shop, and a group of friends I’d had since college. Why would I give that up for humidity and alligators?\n\nI spent two weeks making lists. Pros, cons, more cons. My mom said, “If you don’t go, you’ll always wonder.” My best friend said, “If you go, you’ll miss us.” They were both right, which was not helpful.\n\nIn the end, I took the job, mostly because I was afraid of regretting it. And for the first six months, honestly, I second-guessed it every single day. I missed my friends. I missed snow, which I never thought I’d say. I kept thinking, “If I had stayed, I would be at our Friday trivia night right now.”\n\nBut slowly, things changed. I started going to a run club on Bayshore. I met people at a Spanish class (in Tampa, of all places!). I found a new coffee shop. And one Saturday, I watched the sunset at Pass-a-Grille with my new friends, and I realized I hadn’t thought about Chicago all day.\n\nIn hindsight, the move was the best decision I’ve ever made. If I hadn’t come here, I wouldn’t have met my husband, I wouldn’t have this job, and I wouldn’t know how good a real Cuban sandwich is.\n\nDo I have regrets? A few. I wish I had visited my grandmother more before she passed. I should have learned to cook earlier. But I’ve learned something important: you can’t make decisions to avoid every regret. You make the best choice you can with what you know, and then you make the most of it.\n\nSo if you’re standing in front of a big decision right now, scared, making lists, here’s my advice. Imagine yourself ten years from now. Which choice would you be prouder of? Go with that one.",
