     "type": "read",
     "title": "A toast at Claire’s one-year “Americaversary”",
     "img": "fe:clinking_glasses",
     "text": "Okay, can everyone hear me? Great. I’ll keep this short, I promise. Well, short-ish.\n\nOne year ago today, I landed at Tampa International Airport with two suitcases, a very nervous husband, and English that was… let’s say “a work in progress.” I remember standing at the Publix deli counter for ten minutes, practicing in my head: “Can I get half a pound of turkey?” And when it was finally my turn, the guy smiled and said, “What can I get you, hon?” And I panicked. I blurted out, “I have thirty years!” Don’t ask me why. He just looked at me and said, “Well, happy birthday!” I was mortified.\n\nSo there I was, in a country where nobody understood my jokes, where I couldn’t figure out the tipping, and where it was so hot that, I’m not even kidding, I once saw an iguana fall out of a tree because it was too cold. Florida makes no sense.\n\nBut what I didn’t know back then was how many amazing people I was about to meet. Jess, who knocked on our door with cookies on our second day. Monica, who taught me that “interesting” means “no.” Mike from 204, who still waves at me every single morning. And all of you, who listened patiently while I said “actually” when I meant “currently” about four hundred times.\n\nWhat I love most about this place isn’t the beaches, although the beaches are great. It’s the way people here make you feel welcome, even when you’re different. Never have I felt so far from home and so at home at the same time.\n\nThis year, I got my first job as a hygienist. I learned what a “rain check” is. I survived my first hurricane season. And last week, at the deli counter, I ordered half a pound of turkey without practicing first.\n\nThe guy said, “Anything else, hon?” And I said, “No, I’m good, thanks. Have a good one!”\n\nSo please raise your glasses. To Tampa, to new beginnings, and to all of you. Cheers!",
